// Finance — the spend side of the money the platform already tracks (docs/148).
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. sparx has always been able to answer "what came in": payments,
// payouts, receivables, revenue by channel. It has never been able to answer "did
// we make any money", because the other half of the subtraction lived nowhere. Bits
// of it were captured incidentally — landed cost in `inventory_cost_layers`, supplier
// cost on a dropship order, processor fees on a payout — and the rest (wages, rent,
// subcontractors, fuel, software, insurance) was simply not a thing you could type in.
//
// WHAT THIS IS NOT — and this is the load-bearing constraint, not a phase marker.
// This is NOT bookkeeping. There is no general ledger here, no double entry, no chart
// of accounts, no bank reconciliation, no trial balance, no tax filing, and no payroll
// run. Small businesses do not want a new platform holding their books, and they are
// right not to: that trust takes a decade to earn and QuickBooks and Sage 50 already
// have it. sparx records what a business SPENT and what it EARNED, nets the two, and
// hands a clean, mapped export to whoever actually keeps the books. Anything in a PR
// here that starts to look like an accounting engine is out of scope by construction.
//
// THE ONE RULE THAT PREVENTS THE CLASSIC BUG. An expense row is A BILL THE BUSINESS
// PAID FOR SOMETHING IT CONSUMED. Stock purchases are NOT expenses — a purchase order
// converts cash into inventory VALUE, and that value becomes an expense only when the
// goods sell, at which point `inventory_cost_consumption` already records it. Writing
// a PO into this ledger would double-count every part: once on receipt, again as COGS
// on sale. So the profit rollup at the bottom of this file READS cost of sale from the
// inventory tables and fees from the payment tables; it never copies them in here.
// Materializing a number the platform already owns is how two sources of truth start.
//
// Site scope follows docs/131 §4: expenses belong to a BUSINESS, not a billing
// container. Two unrelated businesses under one owner never share a ledger. Nullable
// property_id because a genuine tenant-level cost exists (the sparx bill covers every
// site) — null is the "not attributable to one business" bucket, shown as such, never
// silently folded into a site's numbers.
//
// All tables are tenant-scoped with FORCE RLS + the canonical `tenant_isolation`
// policy. Owned by @sparx/finance; the rollup is driven by the finance-worker.

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. Categories — the owner's words, with just enough structure to subtract
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// What a business calls the money it spends: "Wages", "Parts", "Subcontractors",
// "Rent", "Fuel", "Software". Deliberately NOT a chart of accounts — a non-technical
// owner does not have one, does not want one, and picking "6420 · Repairs &
// Maintenance" from a list of 180 is how the feature goes unused.
//
// `kind` is the only accounting-shaped thing here, and it earns its place because a
// profit figure is meaningless without it: cost_of_sale subtracts to GROSS profit,
// labor and operating subtract to NET. Three values, chosen once per category by the
// owner, and defaulted correctly on every seeded category so most tenants never touch it.
//
// `exportCode` is where the accountant's vocabulary lives instead: the account name or
// code this category maps to in QuickBooks / Xero / Sage 50. Set by the connector's
// mapping screen (see FinanceAccountingMapping, which owns the per-provider version of
// this); this column is the fallback for a plain CSV export with no connection.

model FinanceExpenseCategory {
  id       String @id @default(dbgenerated("gen_random_uuid()")) @db.Uuid
  tenantId String @map("tenant_id") @db.Uuid

  name String @db.VarChar(120)
  // Stable machine handle for the seeded set (`wages`, `parts`, `rent`, …) so a
  // deriver can find "the wages category" without matching on a name the owner is
  // free to rename. Null for a category the tenant invented.
  slug String? @db.VarChar(60)

  // cost_of_sale | labor | operating. CHECK-constrained in the migration.
  kind String @default("operating") @db.VarChar(20)

  // Owner-facing colour for charts. Null = the rollup assigns one by kind, so a
  // tenant who never opens settings still gets a legible breakdown.
  color String? @db.VarChar(7)

  // Seeded by the module install. A system category cannot be deleted (a deriver
  // targets it by slug) but CAN be renamed and recoloured — the owner's vocabulary
  // wins over ours everywhere it is only a label.
  isSystem Boolean @default(false) @map("is_system")

  // Fallback account name/code for a CSV export with no live connection.
  exportCode String? @map("export_code") @db.VarChar(60)

  sortOrder  Int       @default(0) @map("sort_order")
  archivedAt DateTime? @map("archived_at") @db.Timestamptz

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  tenant     Tenant                    @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  expenses   FinanceExpense[]
  recurring  FinanceRecurringExpense[]
  mappings   FinanceAccountingMapping[]

  @@unique([tenantId, slug], map: "finance_expense_categories_tenant_slug_unique")
  @@index([tenantId, archivedAt])
  @@map("finance_expense_categories")
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. Vendors — who got paid, as one record instead of three spellings
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The plumber, the landlord, the parts wholesaler, the electric company.
//
// Why a table rather than free text on the expense: "spend by vendor" is one of the
// three questions this module exists to answer, and free text answers it wrong the
// first time someone types "Napa" and "NAPA Auto Parts". It is also the unit the
// accounting connectors map on — QuickBooks and Xero both key expenses to a Vendor
// record, so an export without a stable vendor identity lands as unassigned.
//
// Why NOT reuse Supplier or Company: finance must work with inventory AND crm both
// off. A hard FK to a module-owned table would couple a standalone finance tenant to
// modules it never bought. So the links below are deliberately FK-LESS plain uuids —
// set when the tenant does run those modules, so "NAPA the inventory supplier" and
// "NAPA the payee" are the same row on screen without the schema requiring it.

model FinanceVendor {
  id       String @id @default(dbgenerated("gen_random_uuid()")) @db.Uuid
  tenantId String @map("tenant_id") @db.Uuid

  name String @db.VarChar(200)

  // Optional links to the same party as the other modules see it. FK-less on purpose
  // (see above) — the service layer resolves + validates them when those modules are on.
  supplierId String? @map("supplier_id") @db.Uuid
  companyId  String? @map("company_id") @db.Uuid

  email       String? @db.VarChar(255)
  phone       String? @db.VarChar(40)
  website     String? @db.Text
  // Free-form; a payee address is a printed string far more often than it is queried.
  address     String? @db.Text
  accountRef  String? @map("account_ref") @db.VarChar(120)
  // Net 30 and friends — informational here, used to date an unpaid expense's due date.
  paymentTerms String? @map("payment_terms") @db.VarChar(20)

  notes      String?   @db.Text
  archivedAt DateTime? @map("archived_at") @db.Timestamptz

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  tenant    Tenant                    @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  expenses  FinanceExpense[]
  recurring FinanceRecurringExpense[]

  @@index([tenantId, archivedAt])
  @@index([tenantId, supplierId])
  @@map("finance_vendors")
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. The expense — the spine
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// One thing the business paid for. Typed in, generated from a recurring template,
// imported from a bank/accounting CSV, or derived from something sparx already knows
// it charged them (the sparx subscription; a staff member's hours × their rate).
//
// TWO DATES, and they are not the same question. `incurredAt` is when the cost BELONGS
// — the month the rent covers, the day the job used the fuel — and is what every
// profit figure buckets on. `paidAt` is when money actually left, and null means it
// has not yet: an unpaid expense is a payable, which is the mirror of the receivables
// surface that already ships. Bucketing profit on payment date is how a business that
// pays its suppliers on the 1st reports a catastrophic month every month.

model FinanceExpense {
  id       String  @id @default(dbgenerated("gen_random_uuid()")) @db.Uuid
  tenantId String  @map("tenant_id") @db.Uuid
  // The business this cost belongs to. Null = tenant-level, not attributable to one
  // site (the sparx bill). Surfaced as its own bucket, never folded into a site.
  propertyId String? @map("property_id") @db.Uuid

  categoryId String  @map("category_id") @db.Uuid
  vendorId   String? @map("vendor_id") @db.Uuid

  // What it was for, in the owner's words. Required — an unlabelled number in a
  // ledger is a number nobody can act on six months later.
  description String @db.VarChar(300)

  // manual | recurring | imported | labor | sparx_bill | api.
  // CHECK-constrained. Drives whether the row is editable in the UI: a derived row
  // is corrected at its source, not here, or the next derivation run overwrites it.
  source String @default("manual") @db.VarChar(20)

  // What produced a non-manual row, and its id in that system. FK-less by necessity
  // (it spans tables and even external systems) and UNIQUE with tenant — that unique
  // is what makes every deriver and importer IDEMPOTENT. Re-running the labor
  // deriver for a week, or re-importing the same bank CSV, updates rows instead of
  // doubling the month's spend. Null for a hand-typed expense.
  sourceType String? @map("source_type") @db.VarChar(40)
  sourceId   String? @map("source_id") @db.VarChar(200)

  amountCents Int    @map("amount_cents")
  currency    String @default("USD") @db.VarChar(3)

  // Base-currency conversion, same contract as PurchaseOrder: null on a same-currency
  // expense, where storing a rate of 1 would imply a conversion happened.
  fxRate                  Decimal? @map("fx_rate") @db.Decimal(18, 8)
  baseCurrencyAmountCents Int?     @map("base_currency_amount_cents")

  // Tax paid on top, tracked but never CALCULATED here — sparx does not do tax
  // treatment on purchases, it records what the receipt says so the export carries it.
  taxCents Int @default(0) @map("tax_cents")

  incurredAt DateTime  @map("incurred_at") @db.Timestamptz
  paidAt     DateTime? @map("paid_at") @db.Timestamptz
  dueAt      DateTime? @map("due_at") @db.Timestamptz

  // card | bank | cash | check | other. CHECK-constrained. Null on an unpaid expense.
  paymentMethod String? @map("payment_method") @db.VarChar(20)
  // Receipt / bill / cheque number as printed on the document.
  reference     String? @map("reference") @db.VarChar(120)

  // Whether this cost has been handed to the tenant's accounting system yet, and as
  // what. Lets the connector answer "what still needs sending" with an index scan
  // rather than a diff against a remote list.
  exportedAt         DateTime? @map("exported_at") @db.Timestamptz
  externalRef        String?   @map("external_ref") @db.VarChar(200)

  notes    String? @db.Text
  metadata Json    @default("{}")

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz
  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at") @db.Timestamptz

  tenant      Tenant                     @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  // Cascade: an expense is one business's cost. It goes with the site, exactly like
  // that site's revenue does.
  property    Property?                  @relation(fields: [propertyId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  // Restrict: deleting a category that still has spend against it would silently
  // rewrite history. The UI archives instead, and offers to re-file first.
  category    FinanceExpenseCategory     @relation(fields: [categoryId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  vendor      FinanceVendor?             @relation(fields: [vendorId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  allocations FinanceExpenseAllocation[]
  attachments FinanceExpenseAttachment[]

  @@unique([tenantId, sourceType, sourceId], map: "finance_expenses_tenant_source_unique")
  @@index([tenantId, incurredAt])
  @@index([tenantId, propertyId, incurredAt])
  @@index([tenantId, categoryId, incurredAt])
  @@index([tenantId, vendorId])
  // "What have I not paid yet" — the payables mirror of the receivables surface.
  @@index([tenantId, paidAt, dueAt])
  @@index([tenantId, exportedAt])
  @@map("finance_expenses")
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 4. Allocation — which job the money was for
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Job profitability is the reason this module beats a spreadsheet, and it needs to
// survive the messy case: one $480 fuel bill covering three jobs, a $2,000 tool that
// belongs to no job at all.
//
// ONE mechanism, not two. There is deliberately no `orderId` column on the expense
// itself for "the simple case" — a second path is how half the spend ends up
// invisible to the report that joins the other one. The service layer writes a single
// allocation row when the owner picks one job, so the common case is still one click.
//
// Unallocated remainder (expense amount − Σ allocations) is OVERHEAD, and that is a
// real answer rather than a gap: it is the cost of being open, and the rollup reports
// it as such instead of forcing it onto a job that did not incur it.
//
// FK-less target on purpose. An allocation must outlive the record it points at —
// deleting last year's order should not silently rewrite last year's profit — so the
// target carries a denormalized label the UI can still render after the fact.

model FinanceExpenseAllocation {
  id        String @id @default(dbgenerated("gen_random_uuid()")) @db.Uuid
  tenantId  String @map("tenant_id") @db.Uuid
  expenseId String @map("expense_id") @db.Uuid

  // order | booking | customer | product | site. CHECK-constrained.
  targetType String @map("target_type") @db.VarChar(20)
  targetId   String @map("target_id") @db.Uuid
  // What it was called when it was allocated, so the row still reads after a delete.
  targetLabel String? @map("target_label") @db.VarChar(200)

  amountCents Int @map("amount_cents")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  tenant  Tenant         @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  expense FinanceExpense @relation(fields: [expenseId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([expenseId, targetType, targetId], map: "finance_expense_allocations_expense_target_unique")
  // Mapped: Prisma's generated name for this triple truncates to something unreadable
  // at 63 chars, and an index nobody can read is an index nobody checks a plan against.
  @@index([tenantId, targetType, targetId], map: "finance_expense_allocations_tenant_target_idx")
  @@map("finance_expense_allocations")
}

// The receipt. Multiple, because a bill and its proof of payment are two files and an
// owner being audited wants both attached to the same row.

model FinanceExpenseAttachment {
  id        String @id @default(dbgenerated("gen_random_uuid()")) @db.Uuid
  tenantId  String @map("tenant_id") @db.Uuid
  expenseId String @map("expense_id") @db.Uuid
  assetId   String @map("asset_id") @db.Uuid

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz

  tenant  Tenant         @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  expense FinanceExpense @relation(fields: [expenseId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  asset   MediaAsset     @relation(fields: [assetId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([expenseId, assetId], map: "finance_expense_attachments_expense_asset_unique")
  @@index([tenantId, expenseId])
  @@map("finance_expense_attachments")
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 5. Recurring — the costs you already know are coming
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Rent, insurance, the phone bill, three software subscriptions. For most small
// businesses this is the majority of non-payroll operating spend, and it is the same
// number every month — so asking the owner to re-type it twelve times a year is how
// the ledger goes stale by March and the profit figure becomes a lie.
//
// The finance-worker generates the next expense on `nextRunOn`, stamping the new row
// with source='recurring' and sourceId=<template id>:<period>, which is what makes a
// re-run or a replayed event idempotent (see the unique on FinanceExpense).

model FinanceRecurringExpense {
  id         String  @id @default(dbgenerated("gen_random_uuid()")) @db.Uuid
  tenantId   String  @map("tenant_id") @db.Uuid
  propertyId String? @map("property_id") @db.Uuid

  name       String  @db.VarChar(200)
  categoryId String  @map("category_id") @db.Uuid
  vendorId   String? @map("vendor_id") @db.Uuid

  amountCents Int    @map("amount_cents")
  currency    String @default("USD") @db.VarChar(3)

  // weekly | biweekly | monthly | quarterly | annual. CHECK-constrained.
  cadence String @db.VarChar(20)
  // Which day of the month it lands (1–31, clamped to month end). Null for the
  // week-based cadences, which follow `startsOn`'s weekday.
  dayOfMonth Int? @map("day_of_month")

  startsOn DateTime  @map("starts_on") @db.Date
  endsOn   DateTime? @map("ends_on") @db.Date

  // The worker's cursor. Advanced only after the expense row commits, so a crash
  // mid-generation replays rather than skips a month.
  nextRunOn       DateTime? @map("next_run_on") @db.Date
  lastGeneratedOn DateTime? @map("last_generated_on") @db.Date

  // Generate the row automatically, or just remind someone it is due. An owner who
  // wants to see the real invoice before it hits their numbers picks the latter.
  autoGenerate Boolean @default(true) @map("auto_generate")
  isActive     Boolean @default(true) @map("is_active")

  notes String? @db.Text

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  tenant   Tenant                 @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  property Property?              @relation(fields: [propertyId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  category FinanceExpenseCategory @relation(fields: [categoryId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  vendor   FinanceVendor?         @relation(fields: [vendorId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([tenantId, isActive, nextRunOn], map: "finance_recurring_expenses_tenant_active_next_run_idx")
  @@map("finance_recurring_expenses")
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 6. Accounting connections — the handoff, which is the whole trust story
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// sparx does not keep the books; the tenant's accountant does, in QuickBooks or Sage
// 50 or Xero. That is a deliberate product position (see the header), and it only
// works if the handoff is genuinely good — an export nobody can import is the same as
// no export, and it is where "we integrate with QuickBooks" usually turns out to mean
// a CSV with the wrong column order.
//
// Mirrors the per-domain connection pattern already used by ChannelConnection,
// SocialConnection and CalendarConnection: the tenant's own OAuth grant, boxed
// AES-256-GCM, never plaintext, never a platform-level credential.
//
// NOTE for whoever adds the first adapter: `IntegrationCategory` in
// @sparx/integrations does NOT yet carry an `accounting` member, and that is correct
// by that file's own rule — a category is added the day a live adapter dispatches it,
// not the day one is planned. Add it in the same PR as the adapter.

model FinanceAccountingConnection {
  id         String  @id @default(dbgenerated("gen_random_uuid()")) @db.Uuid
  tenantId   String  @map("tenant_id") @db.Uuid
  // Books are kept per business, not per billing container — a bookkeeper for Bob's
  // Parts has no business seeing Savory Donuts' ledger.
  propertyId String? @map("property_id") @db.Uuid

  // quickbooks_online | quickbooks_desktop | xero | sage50 | freshbooks | wave | csv.
  // CHECK-constrained. `csv` is a real provider, not the absence of one: it holds the
  // tenant's column mapping + delimiter choices so a manual export is repeatable.
  provider String @db.VarChar(40)

  // active | expired | revoked | error. Same vocabulary as SocialConnection, for the
  // same reason: a dead grant must surface as "reconnect" before a sync silently stops.
  status String @default("active") @db.VarChar(20)

  // The connected company file, as the vendor names it.
  externalId  String? @map("external_id") @db.VarChar(255)
  displayName String? @map("display_name") @db.VarChar(255)

  accessTokenEnc  String?   @map("access_token_enc") @db.Text
  refreshTokenEnc String?   @map("refresh_token_enc") @db.Text
  tokenExpiresAt  DateTime? @map("token_expires_at") @db.Timestamptz
  scopes          String[]  @default([]) @db.VarChar(120)

  // Which directions this connection runs, and how often. Export is the default and
  // the point; import exists to pull the chart of accounts + vendor list so the
  // mapping screen offers real choices instead of a free-text box.
  syncExpenses Boolean @default(true) @map("sync_expenses")
  syncInvoices Boolean @default(true) @map("sync_invoices")
  syncPayments Boolean @default(true) @map("sync_payments")
  // manual | daily | weekly. CHECK-constrained. Manual means the owner presses the
  // button, which is what a cautious first month looks like.
  syncCadence  String  @default("manual") @map("sync_cadence") @db.VarChar(20)

  // Never send anything dated before this. Set at connect time to the tenant's books
  // "closed through" date — pushing entries into a closed period is the single fastest
  // way to make an accountant distrust the integration permanently.
  syncFromDate DateTime? @map("sync_from_date") @db.Date

  lastSyncAt     DateTime? @map("last_sync_at") @db.Timestamptz
  lastSyncStatus String?   @map("last_sync_status") @db.VarChar(20)
  lastError      Json?     @map("last_error")
  settings       Json      @default("{}")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  tenant   Tenant                     @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  property Property?                  @relation(fields: [propertyId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  mappings FinanceAccountingMapping[]
  syncRuns FinanceAccountingSyncRun[]

  // Hand-edited to NULLS NOT DISTINCT in the migration, same as SocialConnection —
  // otherwise unlimited (tenant, NULL, 'xero') rows coexist.
  @@unique([tenantId, propertyId, provider], map: "finance_accounting_connections_tenant_property_provider_unique")
  @@index([tenantId, status])
  @@map("finance_accounting_connections")
}

// One line of the translation table between sparx's vocabulary and the accountant's.
//
// This is the part that makes an export land clean, and it is per-provider because
// QuickBooks wants an account NAME, Xero wants a numeric code, and Sage 50 wants both.
// Keyed loosely (`sparxType` + `sparxId`) rather than one nullable FK per mappable
// thing, because the mappable set grows — categories today, tax rates and payment
// methods and income accounts next — and a column per concept means a migration every
// time the accountant asks for one more.

model FinanceAccountingMapping {
  id           String @id @default(dbgenerated("gen_random_uuid()")) @db.Uuid
  tenantId     String @map("tenant_id") @db.Uuid
  connectionId String @map("connection_id") @db.Uuid

  // expense_category | tax_rate | payment_method | income_account | vendor.
  // CHECK-constrained.
  sparxType String @map("sparx_type") @db.VarChar(30)
  // The sparx-side id. Uuid-as-text because `payment_method` maps a vocabulary value
  // ('card'), not a row.
  sparxId   String @map("sparx_id") @db.VarChar(200)

  // Typed FK for the one case that has a real table today, so the mapping screen can
  // join instead of resolving ids by hand. Null for the loosely-keyed kinds above.
  categoryId String? @map("category_id") @db.Uuid

  // What it is called on the other side.
  externalId   String  @map("external_id") @db.VarChar(200)
  externalName String? @map("external_name") @db.VarChar(255)
  externalCode String? @map("external_code") @db.VarChar(60)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  tenant     Tenant                      @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  connection FinanceAccountingConnection @relation(fields: [connectionId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  category   FinanceExpenseCategory?     @relation(fields: [categoryId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([connectionId, sparxType, sparxId], map: "finance_accounting_mappings_connection_sparx_unique")
  @@index([tenantId, connectionId])
  @@map("finance_accounting_mappings")
}

// What happened the last time we talked to their accounting system.
//
// Kept as rows rather than a `lastError` blob because the failure that matters is
// almost never the whole run — it is the 3 expenses out of 140 that bounced, and an
// owner needs to see WHICH three and why. `failures` holds them; the counts let the
// UI say "137 sent, 3 need attention" without opening the json.

model FinanceAccountingSyncRun {
  id           String @id @default(dbgenerated("gen_random_uuid()")) @db.Uuid
  tenantId     String @map("tenant_id") @db.Uuid
  connectionId String @map("connection_id") @db.Uuid

  // export | import. CHECK-constrained.
  direction String @db.VarChar(10)
  // running | success | partial | failed. CHECK-constrained. `partial` is a first-class
  // outcome, not an error — see above.
  status    String @default("running") @db.VarChar(20)
  // Which records this run covered: expenses | invoices | payments | accounts | vendors.
  scope     String @db.VarChar(20)

  // Trigger provenance, so a surprise sync can be traced: manual | scheduled | event.
  trigger String @default("manual") @db.VarChar(20)

  periodStart DateTime? @map("period_start") @db.Date
  periodEnd   DateTime? @map("period_end") @db.Date

  recordsTotal   Int @default(0) @map("records_total")
  recordsSynced  Int @default(0) @map("records_synced")
  recordsSkipped Int @default(0) @map("records_skipped")
  recordsFailed  Int @default(0) @map("records_failed")

  // [{ recordId, recordType, code, message }] — the three that bounced.
  failures Json? @map("failures")

  startedAt  DateTime  @default(now()) @map("started_at") @db.Timestamptz
  finishedAt DateTime? @map("finished_at") @db.Timestamptz

  tenant     Tenant                      @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  connection FinanceAccountingConnection @relation(fields: [connectionId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@index([tenantId, connectionId, startedAt], map: "finance_accounting_sync_runs_tenant_connection_started_idx")
  @@map("finance_accounting_sync_runs")
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 7. The rollup — the one number the module exists to produce
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// One row per (tenant, site, UTC day). Follows the Rollup* grain contract already used
// by RollupCommerceDailyRevenue, including the surrogate-PK-plus-NULLS-NOT-DISTINCT
// trick for the nullable property.
//
// Note what is and is not stored. `cogsCents` and `feeCents` are COPIED here at
// rollup time but are OWNED elsewhere — inventory cost consumption and the payment
// tables respectively (see the header rule). This table is a cache of a subtraction,
// so it is safe to truncate and recompute, and the worker's reconcile pass does
// exactly that for any day whose sources changed. Nothing reads a profit figure from
// here without the option to recompute it from source.

model RollupFinanceDailyProfit {
  id         String   @id @default(dbgenerated("gen_random_uuid()")) @db.Uuid
  tenantId   String   @map("tenant_id") @db.Uuid
  propertyId String?  @map("property_id") @db.Uuid
  bucket     DateTime @db.Date

  // Money in, net of refunds. Sourced from the commerce/invoicing revenue rollups so
  // the two surfaces can never disagree about what came in.
  revenueCents BigInt @default(0) @map("revenue_cents")

  // Cost of what was sold that day, from `inventory_cost_consumption` (+ dropship
  // supplier cost for orders that never touched stock).
  cogsCents BigInt @default(0) @map("cogs_cents")
  // Processor + payout fees, from the payment tables.
  feeCents  BigInt @default(0) @map("fee_cents")

  // The three ledger slices, split by category kind. Labor is separated from operating
  // because for a service business it is the largest single number and burying it in
  // "operating" makes the breakdown useless.
  laborCents     BigInt @default(0) @map("labor_cents")
  costOfSaleCents BigInt @default(0) @map("cost_of_sale_cents")
  operatingCents BigInt @default(0) @map("operating_cents")

  // revenue − (cogs + fees + costOfSale) — what the work itself made.
  grossProfitCents BigInt @default(0) @map("gross_profit_cents")
  // gross − (labor + operating) — what the business made.
  netProfitCents   BigInt @default(0) @map("net_profit_cents")

  // Expense spend not tied to any job (Σ expenses − Σ allocations) for the day. Not a
  // separate subtraction — it is already inside the three slices above — but reported
  // on its own so "what does it cost to keep the doors open" is answerable directly.
  unallocatedCents BigInt @default(0) @map("unallocated_cents")

  computedAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("computed_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt  DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  tenant   Tenant    @relation(fields: [tenantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  property Property? @relation(fields: [propertyId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([tenantId, propertyId, bucket], map: "rollup_finance_daily_profit_grain")
  @@index([tenantId, bucket])
  @@map("rollup_finance_daily_profit")
}
